    "explanation": "The infant has progressively poor feeding, absent deep tendon reflexes, tongue fasciculations, generalized weakness, and preserved alertness. These are classic signs of SMA type I, a lower motor neuron disorder. Area A represents the anterior horn cell of the spinal cord, where the pathological motor neuron loss occurs. The SMN1 gene produces survival motor neuron protein; reduced function causes anterior horn cells to degenerate. SMN2 copy number influences disease severity. SMA type I presents early with severe weakness and respiratory insufficiency; types II and III generally begin later and follow a more slowly progressive course. Molecular genetic testing confirms the diagnosis. Creatine kinase may be normal or only mildly elevated, helping distinguish motor neuron disease from primary muscle injury. Guillain–Barré syndrome, infant botulism, and muscular dystrophies have different distributions, time courses, and pathological sites.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) is an autosomal recessive disorder most often due to loss of SMN1 function.\n• Degeneration of spinal anterior horn motor neurons causes profound hypotonia, weakness, and diminished deep tendon reflexes.",
