    "correctAnswerText": "Decrease PEEP from 8 to 5 cm H₂O",
    "explanation": "The infant after tetralogy of Fallot repair has elevated central venous pressure and echocardiographic evidence of poor right ventricular systolic performance. Lowering PEEP from 8 to 5 cm H₂O may reduce intrathoracic pressure, improve preload, and relieve lung-overdistension-related pulmonary vascular resistance. The intervention must be balanced against oxygenation and alveolar recruitment, since inadequate PEEP can also worsen pulmonary vascular resistance via atelectasis. Here chest radiography shows no important atelectasis or edema and oxygenation is sufficient to allow a trial of lower PEEP. Changing the respiratory rate may worsen acid–base status without a direct benefit to preload. Increasing inspiratory time or tidal volume would increase mean airway pressure and could further compromise right ventricular function.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Positive end-expiratory pressure can decrease systemic venous return and right ventricular filling by raising intrathoracic pressure.\n• Lung overdistension can increase pulmonary vascular resistance, raising right ventricular afterload in patients with impaired right ventricular function.",
