    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "correctAnswerText": "Total anomalous pulmonary venous drainage",
    "explanation": "The newborn has tachypnea, cyanosis, hypoxemia poorly responsive to supplemental oxygen, right-sided chamber enlargement, and central venous blood with unexpectedly high oxygen tension. Oxygenated pulmonary venous blood entering the systemic venous circulation explains the elevated central venous PO₂. TAPVR may be supracardiac, cardiac, infracardiac, or mixed. Pulmonary venous obstruction causes severe pulmonary congestion and respiratory failure; unobstructed lesions often produce pulmonary overcirculation and right-sided volume loading. Radiographic findings depend on the subtype and degree of obstruction. Hypoplastic left heart syndrome usually causes systemic outflow failure as ductal flow decreases. Pulmonary atresia with intact septum produces reduced pulmonary blood flow and severe cyanosis. Transposition of the great arteries has parallel systemic and pulmonary circulations requiring a site of mixing; the characteristic systemic venous oxygenation in this vignette favors TAPVR.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR) occurs when pulmonary veins connect to the systemic venous circulation rather than directly to the left atrium.\n• High oxygen tension in central venous blood can indicate mixing with oxygenated pulmonary venous return; a snowman or figure-of-eight chest silhouette may occur in unobstructed supracardiac TAPVR.",
