    "id": 9019,
    "title": "PREP 2026 Block 1 Question 19",
    "scenario": "A 4-year-old child with chronic renal failure has 24 hours of vomiting, irritability, fever, and oliguria. On PICU arrival she is mottled and unresponsive, with distant heart sounds and nonpalpable pulses. The monitor shows wide QRS complexes (110 ms) and heart rate 120/min, as depicted below. Which IV medication is MOST effective immediately?",
    "choices": {
      "A": "Adenosine",
      "B": "Calcium gluconate",
      "C": "Lidocaine",
      "D": "Magnesium sulfate"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "correctAnswerText": "Calcium gluconate",
    "explanation": "The child with renal failure has oliguria, a wide QRS, prominent T waves, poor perfusion, and impending circulatory collapse. Severe hyperkalemia is the likely cause. Elevated extracellular potassium alters the resting membrane potential, eventually impairing sodium channel availability and slowing cardiac conduction. Intravenous calcium gluconate rapidly increases the threshold potential relative to the resting membrane potential, protecting the heart while other interventions lower the potassium concentration. Subsequent measures include insulin with glucose and nebulized beta-agonist to shift potassium intracellularly, as well as potassium removal by diuresis or dialysis when indicated. Adenosine treats selected supraventricular tachycardias, not hyperkalemia. Lidocaine and magnesium sulfate do not correct the potassium-dependent conduction disturbance and should not delay calcium. If the child is truly pulseless, standard pediatric cardiac arrest resuscitation also applies.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Peaked T waves are an early characteristic electrocardiographic finding of hyperkalemia, often with serum potassium around 6–8 mEq/L, although ECG changes are variable.\n• Intravenous calcium stabilizes the myocardium immediately in severe hyperkalemia with ECG changes; it does not remove potassium from the body.",
