    "explanation": "Pleural triglycerides of 950 mg/dL are strongly consistent with chylothorax. With no recent thoracic surgery, a congenital pulmonary or central lymphatic malformation should be considered. Aberrant lymphatic vessels and disrupted lymphatic flow may cause chylothorax, pulmonary interstitial disease, or plastic bronchitis. Magnetic resonance lymphangiography provides a minimally invasive map of lymphatic anatomy and flow, including central conducting pathways, and is the most informative option here. Treatment starts with drainage when respiratory compromise is significant; targeted lymphatic embolization, sclerotherapy, surgery, or medication such as sirolimus may be considered depending on anatomy and underlying disease.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Complex lymphatic anomalies can cause severe pulmonary manifestations requiring critical care.\n• Doppler ultrasound is a useful initial study, but contrast magnetic resonance lymphangiography can define complex central lymphatic malformations.\n• Complex malformations benefit from multidisciplinary care and selected surgical, interventional, and medical treatments.",
