    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "correctAnswerText": "stop sevoflurane",
    "explanation": "Rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia, hyperthermia, and acidosis after exposure to sevoflurane strongly suggest malignant hyperthermia, typically associated with susceptibility involving RYR1-mediated calcium release in skeletal muscle. The first maneuver is to stop the volatile anesthetic and remove it from the breathing circuit, then ventilate with 100% oxygen and rapidly administer intravenous dantrolene (typically 2.5 mg/kg, repeated as needed). Cooling, electrolyte and acid-base management, cardiac monitoring, and surveillance for rhabdomyolysis are essential. Dantrolene is crucial but does not replace immediate discontinuation of the trigger. Avoid succinylcholine and all volatile anesthetics.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Malignant hyperthermia is a life-threatening reaction to volatile anesthetics and/or succinylcholine.\n• Treatment begins by immediately stopping the triggering agent, giving 100% oxygen, administering dantrolene, and providing supportive management of acidosis, hyperkalemia, and hyperthermia.\n• MHAUS offers crisis-management resources and a 24-hour emergency hotline.",
