    "id": 9032,
    "title": "PREP 2026 Block 2 Question 12",
    "scenario": "A previously healthy 6-year-old child with presumed multifocal pneumonia develops respiratory failure requiring intubation. Viral respiratory testing and tracheal cultures are negative. She has received piperacillin-tazobactam and remains ventilated on FiO2 0.50, inspiratory pressure 25 cm H2O, PEEP 10 cm H2O, pressure support 10 cm H2O, and respiratory rate 20/min. She has been intubated for 5 days, receiving fentanyl and vecuronium. Her father reports several months of erythematous scaly papules on the dorsal surfaces of both hands (Figure). Of the following, the MOST specific test to order next is",
    "choices": {
      "A": "antinuclear antibody",
      "B": "creatine kinase",
      "C": "erythrocyte sedimentation rate",
      "D": "rheumatoid factor"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "correctAnswerText": "creatine kinase",
    "explanation": "Scaly erythematous papules over the metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints are Gottron papules, a characteristic cutaneous sign of juvenile dermatomyositis. Severe respiratory failure with negative infectious evaluation raises concern for associated interstitial lung disease. Creatine kinase is the best initial specific screening test among the options for inflammatory myopathy. Muscle weakness may be obscured by sedation and paralysis, and some patients have clinically amyopathic dermatomyositis with normal or minimally elevated enzymes. Myositis-specific antibodies, particularly anti-MDA5 in interstitial lung disease, and MRI or biopsy can support further evaluation. ANA and ESR are nonspecific; rheumatoid factor is not useful for this presentation.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Juvenile dermatomyositis has characteristic heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, and nailfold capillary changes.\n• Interstitial lung disease may occur in juvenile dermatomyositis, including clinically amyopathic disease, and can be severe.\n• Elevated creatine kinase supports the diagnosis of inflammatory myopathy.",
