    "title": "PREP 2026 Block 2 Question 13",
    "scenario": "A 9-year-old child with newly diagnosed Burkitt lymphoma begins chemotherapy. His admission LDH is 6,500 U/L, WBC 120,000/µL, and he was given twice-maintenance isotonic hydration with prophylactic allopurinol. On day 2, he is irritable and nauseated, with BP 85/50 mm Hg and HR 145/min. Potassium is 5.2 mEq/L, phosphorus 9.0 mg/dL, calcium 8.0 mg/dL, uric acid 18 mg/dL, BUN 44 mg/dL, creatinine 1.9 mg/dL, pH 7.28, bicarbonate 16 mEq/L, and ECG shows normal QRS and T waves. Of the following, the MOST appropriate next step in management is",
    "choices": {
      "A": "calcium gluconate",
      "B": "continuous renal replacement therapy",
      "C": "rasburicase",
      "D": "sodium bicarbonate"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "correctAnswerText": "rasburicase",
    "explanation": "Tumor lysis syndrome results from rapid malignant cell breakdown with release of uric acid precursors, potassium, and phosphate. Typical findings include hyperuricemia, hyperphosphatemia, hyperkalemia, secondary hypocalcemia, and acute kidney injury. Burkitt lymphoma and high LDH make this patient high risk. Allopurinol limits new uric acid production but does not remove existing uric acid. Rasburicase converts uric acid to soluble allantoin and promptly treats severe hyperuricemia. Check G6PD status when feasible and avoid rasburicase in G6PD deficiency. Calcium is generally reserved for symptomatic hypocalcemia or dangerous ECG effects; bicarbonate is not routinely recommended, and renal replacement therapy is used for refractory derangements or fluid overload.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Rasburicase is first-line treatment for established tumor lysis syndrome with marked hyperuricemia.\n• Continuous renal replacement therapy is reserved for refractory metabolic abnormalities, severe AKI, or fluid overload.\n• Rasburicase is contraindicated in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency due to the risk of hemolysis and methemoglobinemia.",
