    "id": 9036,
    "title": "PREP 2026 Block 2 Question 16",
    "scenario": "A 4-month-old full-term infant has persistent tachypnea from birth, chronic supplemental oxygen requirement (0.25 L/min), and recurrent respiratory admissions with negative infectious and newborn screening evaluation. He develops severe diffuse pulmonary infiltrates, refractory hypoxemia and hypercapnia, requires 4 weeks of venovenous ECMO, and then remains ventilator-dependent despite antibiotics, azithromycin, and systemic steroids. Chest radiograph is shown (Figure). Of the following, the diagnostic study MOST likely to yield the underlying cause of his illness is",
    "choices": {
      "A": "bone marrow biopsy",
      "B": "flow cytometry",
      "C": "genetic analysis",
      "D": "ventilation perfusion scan"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "correctAnswerText": "genetic analysis",
    "explanation": "Persistent unexplained respiratory failure beginning in the neonatal period and unresponsive to standard infectious treatment suggests childhood interstitial lung disease, particularly a genetic surfactant disorder. Surfactant is produced by type II alveolar epithelial cells and lowers surface tension at the alveolar interface. Genetic analysis may identify defects in surfactant protein B or C, ABCA3 transport, or NKX2-1/TTF-1. SFTPB and severe ABCA3 variants may present early with refractory respiratory failure; other variants can present later or in recurrent episodes. High-resolution CT helps characterize lung disease, but genetic testing is most likely to reveal its underlying cause among the listed choices.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Childhood interstitial lung disease comprises diverse parenchymal conditions, many with genetic causes.\n• Inherited dysfunction of surfactant production and transport is an important cause of severe infant lung disease.\n• Relevant genes include SFTPB, SFTPC, ABCA3, and NKX2-1 (thyroid transcription factor 1).",
