    "scenario": "A 2-year-old child suffers an out-of-hospital cardiac arrest after reportedly being well earlier in the day. Bystander CPR lasts 10 minutes and EMS continues for an additional 25 minutes before return of spontaneous circulation. Gastric material is suctioned from the airway. In the PICU, temperature is 34°C, BP 145/95 mm Hg, heart rate 80/min, Glasgow Coma Scale score 3T, and pupils are sluggish. Head CT demonstrates diffuse cerebral edema and herniation (Figures 1 and 2). Of the following, the intervention with the BEST potential to improve clinical outcome is",
    "choices": {
      "A": "hypertonic saline to maintain serum sodium above 155 mOsm/kg",
      "B": "mechanical ventilation to maintain PaCO2 of 30 to 35 mm Hg",
      "C": "sedation and paralysis to maintain intracranial pressure below 20 mm Hg",
      "D": "targeted temperature management to maintain temperature of 36 to 37.5°C"
    },
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "correctAnswerText": "targeted temperature management to maintain temperature of 36 to 37.5°C",
    "explanation": "This child has profound hypoxic-ischemic brain injury after a prolonged resuscitation, with severe cerebral edema on CT. Evidence-based post-arrest care emphasizes maintaining adequate perfusion, oxygenation and ventilation while preventing secondary neurologic injury. Targeted temperature management that avoids fever, including controlled normothermia at 36–37.5°C, is the best choice. Post-arrest hyperthermia is associated with worse neurologic outcomes. Protocols may use controlled hypothermia followed by normothermia in selected settings, but unregulated hyperthermia must be avoided. Strategies derived from severe traumatic brain injury, such as specific ICP targets or routine hyperosmolar therapy, have not shown the same proven outcome benefit in primary anoxic injury.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Temperature control and prevention of fever are important after pediatric cardiac arrest with hypoxic brain injury.\n• Hyperosmolar therapy and intracranial pressure management are used for selected patients with traumatic brain injury.\n• Routine invasive ICP monitoring or targeting a specific ICP after hypoxic-ischemic arrest is not supported by sufficient outcome data.",
