    "explanation": "Persistent fever, pancytopenia, hepatitis, markedly elevated ferritin, hypertriglyceridemia, hypofibrinogenemia, and coagulopathy are highly suggestive of hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH). This syndrome involves pathologic macrophage and T-cell activation with severe systemic inflammation. HLH-2004 diagnostic criteria include fever; splenomegaly; cytopenias in at least two cell lines; hypertriglyceridemia and/or hypofibrinogenemia; histologic hemophagocytosis; low natural-killer-cell activity; elevated ferritin; and increased soluble CD25 (IL-2 receptor). Soluble IL-2 receptor is therefore the strongest confirmatory test among the given choices. Bone marrow hemophagocytosis may be absent or nonspecific and is not mandatory. Management requires urgent specialist-led immunomodulatory treatment and evaluation of an infectious, malignancy-related, or genetic trigger.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH) is a severe dysregulated immune activation syndrome with overlapping clinical presentations.\n• HLH can cause extreme hyperinflammation and multiorgan dysfunction.\n• Diagnosis is supported by meeting at least 5 of 8 standard criteria, which include elevated soluble IL-2 receptor; tissue hemophagocytosis is not required.",
