    "correctAnswerText": "increased antidiuretic hormone secretion",
    "explanation": "The finding of low serum sodium and osmolality, paired with concentrated urine and elevated urine sodium in an apparently euvolemic patient, is consistent with SIADH after major spinal surgery. ADH (arginine vasopressin) acts at collecting-duct V2 receptors, increasing aquaporin-2 insertion and water reabsorption. Surgery, pain, nausea, physiologic stress, and fluid administration can cause nonosmotic ADH secretion. Reduced urine output may be an early sign. Decreased ADH would cause dilute polyuria and hypernatremia, while isolated renal salt wasting generally produces hypovolemia. Evaluate symptomatic hyponatremia promptly, distinguish competing causes, and institute carefully monitored treatment appropriate to symptom severity.\n\nPREP Pearls:\n• Major postoperative stress, especially after spinal fusion, increases the risk of syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH).\n• SIADH causes free-water retention, hypotonic hyponatremia, and inappropriately concentrated urine in a clinically euvolemic patient.\n• Postoperative oliguria from SIADH must be differentiated from hypovolemia by clinical volume assessment and paired serum/urine studies.",
